From 091fc99fa59d37714cc1dac119e866d4f6405db6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:14:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0249 -- 0246's large reads on the DOS handle
 are on by default (DOSAGS_READDIRECT=0 kills)

Round Y on the 486 (S16-IOARMS2, same binary, env-only arms) read 0246
as registered: it HOLDS -- first-play asset reads saved 9.95 ms summed
over the four clips both OFF cells played (music0 -6.65, sound3 -1.31,
sound1 -1.20, sound2 -0.79 ms), which clears the registered falsifier
(25% of the summed prediction midpoints, 20.13 ms) -- and its arm was
audio clean by the registered comparative rule. The saving is below the
predicted bands: about 0.4-0.5 ms per KB of overshoot removed, roughly
half the 0.75-1.0 ms/KB that was registered. Every clip saved, in the
predicted direction.

Exactness does not depend on the target: the lab gates showed identical
bytes for every package section with it on and off (S32P 14/14, S16
15/15) and a table of end-of-file, ungetc, text-mode, buffered and
seek-into-buffer cases that match fread or take it (8/8). One listed
difference remains, and only on a DOS read error: the bypass returns 0
with the FILE's error flag set, where fread could first return the bytes
of buffers that did fill.

DOSAGS_READDIRECT=0 restores the fread path for every read.
---
 Common/util/filestream.cpp | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Common/util/filestream.cpp b/Common/util/filestream.cpp
index 3990202c3..c7312ca68 100644
--- a/Common/util/filestream.cpp
+++ b/Common/util/filestream.cpp
@@ -38,7 +38,7 @@ long long dos_port_file_rd_calls = 0;
 // fread could first return the bytes of buffers that did fill; that is
 // the one difference, and only on a disk error. Only reads over 8 KB
 // (BufferedStream's direct reads; its 8 KB refills stay on fread) and
-// only binary-mode handles. Default OFF: DOSAGS_READDIRECT=1 turns it on.
+// only binary-mode handles. Default ON since patch 0249; DOSAGS_READDIRECT=0 restores the fread path.
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
@@ -52,7 +52,7 @@ static bool dos_readdirect_ok(FILE *f)
     if (dos_port_readdirect_mode < 0)
     {
         const char *e = getenv("DOSAGS_READDIRECT");
-        dos_port_readdirect_mode = (e != nullptr && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0;
+        dos_port_readdirect_mode = (e != nullptr && e[0] == '0') ? 0 : 1;   /* default ON, =0 kills (patch 0249) */
     }
     return dos_port_readdirect_mode == 1 && f->_cnt == 0 && (f->_flag & _IOREAD) &&
         !(f->_flag & (_IOWRT | _IORW | _IOUNGETC | _IOEOF | _IOERR)) && (__file_handle_modes[fileno(f)] & O_BINARY);
